  <Fragment slot="en">
    <p class="mt-6">Thanks for using {b.serviceName} (the "Service"), operated by {b.name} (the "Company", "we"). These terms set out how the Service works and the rights and duties between you and us.</p>

    <h2 id="en-1">1. What YEONON is</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>YEONON is a <b>platform that connects</b> international travelers with Korean local friends ("jigis") who share their taste, so they can find each other and arrange a meetup.</li>
      <li>We do not plan, sell or arrange travel products. Jigis are not our employees, agents or professional tour guides. A jigi's "route" is a neighborhood walk they'd like to share; travelers pick a route and time they like and send a meetup request.</li>
      <li>Members decide for themselves what happens during a meetup (conversation, walking, buying food or drinks) and pay their own costs. We do not handle money between members.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 id="en-2">2. Definitions</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Member: anyone who agrees to these terms and signs up with a Google account — a traveler, jigi or admin.</li>
      <li>Traveler: a member who browses routes and requests meetups.</li>
      <li>Jigi: a member approved after our review (application, interview and identity check) who posts routes and available times.</li>
      <li>Admin: a person we appoint to run the Service.</li>
      <li>Request / booking: a traveler's request for a specific route and time, and its status.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="en-3">3. These terms and changes</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>These terms are posted in the Service and apply once you agree to them when you sign up.</li>
      <li>We may change these terms within the law. We'll post the change and its effective date at least 7 days in advance (30 days for changes that are important or less favorable to you) and ask signed-in members to agree again.</li>
      <li>If you don't agree to the changes, you can delete your account.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 id="en-4">4. Eligibility and accounts</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>You must be <b>18 or older</b>. We ask you to confirm this when you sign up and may restrict accounts where it isn't true.</li>
      <li>You sign up with Google only and choose your role (traveler or jigi) the first time. You can't change your role yourself later. Only we can grant the admin role.</li>
      <li>Don't lend or transfer your account. You're responsible for it — tell us right away if you think someone else is using it.</li>
      <li>Your display name is shown to the people you meet. Don't impersonate others or use offensive names.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 id="en-5">5. Jigi responsibilities</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Jigis must apply truthfully and pass our interview and identity check (done during the interview; we don't collect ID copies) before hosting.</li>
      <li>Meetups always start in a <b>public place</b> such as a café or park entrance. Jigis must not lead travelers to private homes or isolated places.</li>
      <li>Routes and profiles must reflect real experience, without exaggeration or other people's photos or text. Routes are reviewed before they go public, and we may ask for changes or take them down.</li>
      <li>Jigis reply to requests within the deadline and keep confirmed meetups. If they must cancel, they do so in the Service as early as possible.</li>
      <li>Jigis must not use a traveler's information (including chats) for anything other than preparing the meetup, or share it with others.</li>
      <li>Jigis follow our activity guidelines on safety, language and alcohol.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 id="en-6">6. Traveler responsibilities</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Give accurate details (group size, notes) when you request, and be on time for confirmed meetups.</li>
      <li>Don't treat your jigi as a tour guide or staff member, or ask for things outside the Service (driving, accommodation, buying things for you).</li>
      <li>Look after your own safety. In an emergency, call 112 (police) or 119 (fire and ambulance) first.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 id="en-7">7. What's not allowed</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Asking for or pushing payment outside YEONON, or asking for money or goods in return for a meetup</li>
      <li>Collecting contact details or moving people to outside messengers for sales, promotion or anything other than preparing the meetup</li>
      <li>Harassment, sexual remarks, discrimination or hate speech, threats, stalking or anything that makes the other person feel unsafe</li>
      <li>Signing up or applying with false information, or impersonating someone</li>
      <li>Commercial promotion, advertising, pyramid schemes, or taking money to bring people to a business</li>
      <li>Illegal acts or anything against public order</li>
      <li>Scraping the Service or members' information, or interfering with our systems</li>
    </ul>
    <p>If a chat message looks like it shares contact details or asks for outside payment, a safety notice is shown. The message is still delivered, but repeated cases can lead to restrictions.</p>

    <h2 id="en-8">8. Requests, confirmation and reply deadlines</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Travelers request an open time on a route. The time is held as soon as you request it, so no one else can take it.</li>
      <li>If the jigi uses "confirm after accepting", the meetup is confirmed when they accept. With "instant confirm", it's confirmed right away.</li>
      <li>The reply deadline is 12 hours by default, or 2 hours if the meetup is less than 24 hours away. The actual deadline is shown on your request. With no reply in time, the request <b>expires automatically</b> and the time opens up again.</li>
      <li>You can request up to 2 hours before the start, and have up to 3 requests waiting for a reply at once.</li>
      <li>A chat opens between you as soon as a request is made. Before confirmation, please keep to questions about the route.</li>
      <li>These numbers may change as we run the Service; we'll show any change in the Service.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 id="en-9">9. Cancellations and no-shows</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Both travelers and jigis can cancel in the Service before the meetup by choosing a reason. The other person is notified.</li>
      <li>Cancellations within 24 hours of the start are recorded as "late" and may count toward a jigi's quality score and decisions about restrictions.</li>
      <li>Not showing up without notice hurts the other person. You can report no-shows through reviews or reports, and repeated no-shows can lead to restrictions.</li>
      <li>Because there is no payment, there are no refunds or cancellation fees.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 id="en-10">10. Early member discount and fees</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Routes show a list price. Right now an <b>early member discount of 100%</b> applies, so meetups cost ₩0, and the Service has no payment feature.</li>
      <li>We set and show who qualifies, the discount rate, period and limits, and we may change or end the discount. We'll give at least 30 days' notice before the discount ends or fees start, and confirmed bookings keep the terms they were confirmed under.</li>
      <li>If we add payments, we'll add payment and refund terms here and ask you to agree again.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 id="en-11">11. AI recommendations and translation</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Route recommendations, the "why this fits you" text and chat translation use AI. AI output is <b>for reference only</b> and may be inaccurate or incomplete — please check route details yourself before deciding.</li>
      <li>We don't send AI providers personal identifiers like your name, email or contact details. See the <a href="/privacy#en">Privacy notice</a>.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 id="en-12">12. Reviews</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>After a meetup ends, each side can leave one review per booking and edit or delete it within 24 hours of posting.</li>
      <li>Travelers' reviews of jigis may be shown publicly with the rating, text and your first initial. The "something felt unsafe" check is never public and goes only to our team.</li>
      <li>We may hide or remove reviews that are false, defamatory or include personal information.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 id="en-13">13. Reports, blocking, restrictions and suspension</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>You can report or block the other person from the chat menu. A block stops both sides from messaging in that chat.</li>
      <li>We only open a chat when it has been reported, only to handle the report, and every view is logged.</li>
      <li>If a member breaks these terms (especially section 7), we may warn them, hide their routes or suspend the account depending on the case. Where safety is at risk, we may suspend first and explain afterwards. Suspended members can appeal by emailing us.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 id="en-14">14. Deleting your account</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>You can delete your account at any time from your account page. Open requests and bookings are cancelled automatically and the other person is notified.</li>
      <li>Your information is then deleted or anonymized as described in the Privacy notice. This can't be undone.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 id="en-15">15. Our responsibility</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>We provide a way for members to find each other and arrange meetups; we are not a party to the meetup itself. We're not responsible for disputes or losses between members during a meetup unless caused by our intent or gross negligence.</li>
      <li>We take safety steps — interviews and identity checks before approving jigis, route reviews and report handling — but we can't guarantee every member's behavior or the accuracy of everything members post.</li>
      <li>We're not responsible when we can't provide the Service for reasons outside our control, such as natural disasters, network failures, or outages at sign-in, hosting or AI providers.</li>
      <li>Nothing here limits responsibility we can't exclude under applicable law.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 id="en-16">16. Disputes</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Members should first try to resolve disputes between themselves. We can help where needed, for example by checking reports and records.</li>
      <li>Send questions or complaints to <a href={`mailto:${b.email}`}>{b.email}</a> and we'll get back to you.</li>
      <li>These terms are governed by the laws of the Republic of Korea. Court: {legalValue(LEGAL.court, 'en')}.</li>
    </ol>

